  pages={692-698},
  abstract={To address the issues of privacy and environmental adaptability in vision-based gait emotion recognition, a contactless emotion recognition method based on WiFi CSI signals is proposed. The method models the raw CSI sequence as a spatiotemporal graph structure and utilizes a multi-scale adaptive graph convolutional network to achieve structured modeling of gait emotions. The experimental data is collected from a self-built CSI acquisition platform and is heterogeneously aligned with the publicly available Emotion-Gait dataset to generate emotion-labeled fused gait data. During the graph modeling process, temporal and frequency edges are combined to construct the adjacency matrix, capturing both local and global temporal dependencies, while an attention mechanism is used to adaptively integrate multi-scale graph convolution results. Experimental results show that the proposed method significantly outperforms traditional models such as CNN, LSTM, ST-GCN, and existing gait emotion recognition models like GaitNet, T-GER, and Fusion Networks in terms of accuracy, F1 score, and mAP, achieving an accuracy of 90.23%. The study demonstrates that WiFi CSI signals effectively capture emotion-related features, and through structural modeling and scale-enhancement mechanisms, significantly improve the performance of contactless emotion recognition.},
