  pages={74736-74750},
  abstract={Human Activity Recognition (HAR) using Wi-Fi Channel State Information (CSI) is a privacy-preserving alternative to camera-based systems. However, its deployment in complex, multipath-rich environments like classrooms remains challenging due to signal variability and a lack of robust generalization. In this work, we present a comprehensive study on the generalization capabilities of Deep Learning models for CSI-based sensing in educational settings. To lower the barrier to entry, we release a complete open-source platform, including low-cost ESP32 acquisition software, training scripts, and a novel dataset comprising 27 spatial-activity scenarios (9 discrete locations  $\times 3$  activity states) across 5 subjects. Rather than proposing a purely architectural advancement, our primary contribution is a rigorous investigation across three increasing levels of difficulty: (A) a randomized baseline sanity check, (B) strict temporal drift, and (C) leave-one-subject-out (LOSO) cross-user validation. Our results demonstrate that while standard models achieve near-perfect accuracy under ideal conditions, they degrade significantly as temporal and biometric constraints tighten. Crucially, our results indicate that a hybrid architecture combining a 2D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) with an Attention-enhanced Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) achieves the highest overall accuracy. However, our investigation equally positions the standalone BiLSTM as a strong, computationally simpler competitor, offering commendable resilience against the noise and domain shifts inherent in commercial IoT deployments.},
  keywords={Modeling;Wireless fidelity;Bidirectional long short term memory;Human activity recognition;Architecture;Computer architecture;Accuracy;Convolutional neural networks;Internet of Things;Training;Wi-Fi CSI;human activity recognition;deep learning},
  doi={10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3693596},
